<p>The United States and Iran will hold a second round of indirect talks in Geneva on Tuesday, February 17, focused on Tehran’s nuclear programme. Both sides are signalling interest in a deal, while also preparing for escalation if negotiations fail. President Trump said he would be involved “indirectly” and that he believes Iran wants an agreement, but he also warned of “consequences” if there is no deal. The diplomacy is unfolding alongside a sharper military posture. US reporting cited officials saying Washington has sent a second aircraft carrier to the Middle East and is preparing for the possibility of a sustained military campaign if talks break down. Iran, meanwhile, launched IRGC live-fire drills near the Strait of Hormuz—a strategic shipping lane—and cast its position as seeking a “fair and equitable deal” without yielding to threats. One core dispute remains uranium enrichment on Iranian soil. The US has pushed to end it, while Iran says it will not accept a “zero enrichment” demand and has tied any flexibility to sanctions relief. Still, the talks are back on after an initial meeting in Oman described as a “good start,” keeping a narrow but real diplomatic opening alive—one that could lower the risk of conflict and bring more predictability to the region and energy markets.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/CAnqjfnlQWH2NvBJZbEKPcPDI9wgnS3pj-wX2xr_8fyzBoFX6y5OAZr66RjmsObJhxnKqG458OsoUK22PXPDVruqU4qmgMKbrJyYk4rDDPHI' alt='US President Donald Trump speaks to reporters aboard Air Force One on February 16, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Tehran’s signal: Iranian officials cited in Al-Monitor voiced cautious optimism that Washington’s position has become “more realistic,” describing the renewed talks as a way to reduce the risk of US military action if diplomacy fails.</li><li>IAEA meeting: Ahead of Tuesday’s talks, Iran’s foreign minister met IAEA chief Rafael Grossi in Geneva and said he had “real ideas” for a deal.</li><li>Wider agenda: Some coverage says the disputes go beyond the nuclear file to missiles and Iran’s regional proxies, making any agreement harder to reach.</li><li>Energy risk signal: An expert cited by Semafor said Iran’s Strait of Hormuz drills are meant to underline that a war could bring serious consequences for global energy markets.</li></ul><blockquote>I am in Geneva with real ideas to achieve a fair and equitable deal. What is not on the table: submission before threats. - Abbas Araghchi</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Donald Trump: Trump said he will be involved “indirectly” in the Geneva talks and argued Iran wants a deal, while warning of “consequences” if it does not. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1973872/trump-says-he-will-be-involved-indirectly-in-iran-talks'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Iran (Majid Takht-Ravanchi, deputy foreign minister): Takht-Ravanchi told the BBC that Iran is open to compromise if the US is willing to discuss lifting sanctions, saying the ball is “in America’s court” to show sincerity. (<a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-02-16-0800/middle-east-iran-says-us-must-prove-they-want-do-deal-nuclear-talks-geneva'>AllSides</a>)</li><li>Iran (foreign minister Abbas Araghchi): Araghchi cast Iran’s goal as a “fair and equitable deal,” while insisting Iran will not submit to threats. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/iran-meets-un-nuclear-watchdog-ahead-of-u-s-talks'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>US officials (as reported): US officials told Reuters that the US military is preparing for the possibility of a sustained campaign if talks fail. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1973872/trump-says-he-will-be-involved-indirectly-in-iran-talks'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Iran’s IRGC (state media framing): Iranian state TV said the IRGC drills in the Strait of Hormuz are meant to prepare for “potential security and military threats”. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/irans-revolutionary-guards-begin-military-drills-in-strait-of-